course: dictionary
lesson: 1269
title: Bushy: thick and full
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Bushy describes something that grows thickly, so it looks full and fluffy. It is used for hair, fur, leaves and even land. «A squirrel's bushy tail helps it balance and works as a blanket and an umbrella.» The clues are balance, blanket and umbrella, so a bushy tail must be big, thick and fluffy.
explain: Use the swap check. Put «thick and fluffy» in place of bushy: «A squirrel's thick and fluffy tail helps it balance» still makes sense, so your meaning is right. Now try «thin and bare» instead, and the sentence stops making sense as a blanket and an umbrella.
explain: Bushy can also mean covered with bushes, as in «bushy land». When you meet a word with more than one meaning, look at the rest of the sentence to choose the right one. Hair, fur and leaves point to thickness; a place or land points to bushes.
fact: Bushy means growing thickly, with lots of hair, fur or leaves.
fact: «Bushy» can also mean covered with bushes, as in bushy land.
fact: Bushy is an adjective, so its forms are bushier and bushiest.
fact: «Bright-eyed and bushy-tailed» is a phrase that means lively and keen.
fact: «The Arctic fox sleeps with its bushy tail over its nose.» Here bushy describes a thick, fluffy tail.
exercise: «A squirrel's bushy tail helps it balance and works as a blanket and an umbrella.» What does bushy mean here? | Wet and cold | *Thick and fluffy | Short and smooth | Thin and bare
exercise: «The Arctic fox has small ears, short legs and very thick fur.» Find and copy one word that means nearly the same as bushy. | short | *thick | small | legs
exercise: Mia says a bushy beard is neat and trimmed. What is wrong with her idea? | *Bushy means thick and full, so a bushy beard is not neat | Bushy means smelly | Bushy means neat and trimmed | Bushy means tiny and short
test: What does bushy land mean? | Land that is very cold | Land made of sand | Land with no trees at all | *Land covered with bushes
test: Which word could replace bushy in «a bushy tail» with almost the same meaning? | short | *thick | bare | pale
exam: Which is the comparative form of bushy? | bushyer | bushily | bushiest | *bushier
